<h2>The factual knot</h2>
<p>The parties&rsquo; accounts could hardly have differed more. The plaintiff said he was taken to the Registrar&rsquo;s office on 20 February 2009 to sign as a witness and discovered later that a marriage had been registered; the parties, he said, never lived together. The respondent said the parties agreed to marry and registered the marriage before friends, and that dowry demands followed. What ultimately decided the appeal was neither narrative, but a question of status: was the plaintiff a Hindu at all on the date of registration?</p>

<h2>Proving religion in court</h2>
<p>The Bench&rsquo;s treatment of the conversion evidence deserves attention. The trial court had seized on &ldquo;two dates&rdquo; of baptism as a contradiction; the Bench found they were two baptisms &mdash; Catholic in 1998, Pentecostal in 2006 &mdash; each supported by certificates, including those of the plaintiff&rsquo;s parents, and consistent cross-examination. Equally significant is what the court refused to count: names and entries in Aadhaar or ration cards do not fix a person&rsquo;s faith. Religious identity is a matter of belief and practice, proved like any other fact.</p>

<h2>Section 5 is a gate, Section 8 only a register</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>The registration view.</strong> The certificate exists, the parties signed, Column 6 was marked &mdash; so a marriage exists unless fraud is proved.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>The statutory view.</strong> Section 5 permits solemnisation only &ldquo;between any two Hindus&rdquo;. Registration under Section 8 presupposes a marriage the Act recognises; it cannot validate one it does not. An interfaith couple&rsquo;s route is the Special Marriage Act, 1954.</div>
</div>
<p>The respondent&rsquo;s own cross-examination conceded that no marriage ceremony took place and that the affirmative entry was made &ldquo;for registration purposes&rdquo;. With no solemnisation and no capacity under Section 5, there was nothing for the register to record: the Bench declared the entry at Serial No. 157 of 2009 null and void.</p>

<h2>Why the case matters beyond Chennai</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For couples.</strong> An interfaith marriage solemnised and registered under the wrong statute is void &mdash; with consequences for succession, maintenance claims and criminal complaints premised on marriage. The Special Marriage Act route, though slower, is the legally durable one.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For registrars.</strong> The judgment&rsquo;s circulation directions signal closer scrutiny of declarations of religion at registration &mdash; false entries produce litigation measured in decades.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For litigators.</strong> Nullity declarations of registered marriages are fought on status and ceremony; documentary proof of religion and of (non-)solemnisation outweighs the certificate itself.</div>
</div>
<p>Sixteen years elapsed between registration and the declaration of nullity &mdash; a caution, in any jurisdiction, about how long a paper marriage can shadow a life.</p>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment of the Madras High Court for general information only. It is not legal advice and does not comment on any pending proceeding.</div>
